ng  themselves and their their editor was  trying to tell them that to believe in  themselves so that was nice that it was  a little different story arc and then  the other thing that struck me is you're  very used to seeing reporters going  after sources and sources don't want to  talk you know or they're a little cagey  and they say follow the money but they  won't tell you  anything beyond that and here sources  wanted to talk and there were a lot of  what you'd call leaks and you realize  that when good people in the government  are seeing something that they think is  bad they want to tell people and they  need people who will listen so  unfortunately that's not as great for  drama when sources are willing to give  up the information rather easily but  Richard Schiff goes into that that  restaurant and he wants that story told  so and Matthew from your point of view  because there is a legacy at heritage of  newspaper movies what were you looking  for in this one in particular in the  script and the approach that would make  it stand out as it does well I mean a  couple of the other Brooke you know  films you talk about all the President's  Men and spotlight that's really pointing  to and celebrating the success of  journalism as a whole there wasn't a  truth to be known and these guys dug it  up the the in this case its points out a  huge failing of journalism across the  board except for four people and the  fact that we went to war on a lie like  this it's it's it's I mean it's  breathtaking and when you look at this  and you you somehow get dulled to the  events that happen but I mean if you  cast your mind back it's amazing that it  happened so you know it was an  incredible story from all of our  perspectives and one that I really  thought needed to be told what do you  want people to come away from this film  thinking or maybe more importantly doing  well what I want L you what I want ya  vote is the big thing this is this is  the this is a strange time 